  "doctrine": [],
  "note": "Both occurrences of the ankle noun belong to versions of one song. Its Aramaic and Akkadian parallels identify the joint. Watson’s Ugaritic and South Semitic comparisons differ in their consonants or in which joint they denote. A common origin for the ankle, clasp and stooping terms is uncertain. Keros names a returning temple-servant family in Ezra 2:44 and Nehemiah 7:47; neither list connects the name with any of these meanings."
